1
00:00:06,117 --> 00:00:09,583
If there is no Given, the
opposite answer beckons.

2
00:00:09,663 --> 00:00:12,980
Perhaps experience is
concept through and through.

3
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:16,517
Perhaps the world is simply
what our concepts make of it.

4
00:00:17,342 --> 00:00:21,406
The most careful modern form of
this thought is John McDowell's.

5
00:00:21,486 --> 00:00:26,189
Concepts, he argued, are already
at work in perception itself.

6
00:00:26,269 --> 00:00:28,866
That is how experience
can give us reasons.

7
00:00:28,946 --> 00:00:32,072
The conceptual, he wrote, is unbounded.

8
00:00:32,152 --> 00:00:35,795
There is no outer edge where
something non-conceptual waits.

9
00:00:35,875 --> 00:00:39,817
Readers often sum this up as
"conceptual all the way down",

10
00:00:39,897 --> 00:00:42,783
though the phrase is a gloss, not his own.

11
00:00:43,602 --> 00:00:45,402
Hubert Dreyfus objected.

12
00:00:45,491 --> 00:00:48,020
Think of skilled, absorbed activity.

13
00:00:48,100 --> 00:00:52,028
The chess master who sees the
move is not applying concepts.

14
00:00:52,108 --> 00:00:53,903
The expert simply copes.

15
00:00:53,983 --> 00:00:58,364
Their exchange is one of the most
instructive in recent philosophy.

16
00:00:59,169 --> 00:01:03,029
Madhyamaka, the Middle
Way, sides with neither.

17
00:01:03,109 --> 00:01:05,203
McDowell is no idealist.

18
00:01:05,283 --> 00:01:08,409
He insists the world
constrains what we think.

19
00:01:08,489 --> 00:01:11,453
But a mind whose concepts
reach everywhere,

20
00:01:11,533 --> 00:01:16,276
read as an account of what reality is,
swaps the realist's substance for the

21
00:01:16,356 --> 00:01:18,015
idealist's concept.

22
00:01:18,095 --> 00:01:23,831
Read the same way, Dreyfus's absorbed
coping restores a floor beneath concepts.

23
00:01:23,911 --> 00:01:28,002
The Given comes back, in practical
dress rather than perceptual.

24
00:01:28,082 --> 00:01:30,221
Each makes one side come first.

25
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:34,288
What remains is a
symmetry, with two halves.

26
00:01:35,173 --> 00:01:38,775
First, appearance is not
raw material for concepts.

27
00:01:38,855 --> 00:01:41,112
Ice stings with its own sharpness.

28
00:01:41,192 --> 00:01:42,851
No one decides the sting.

29
00:01:42,931 --> 00:01:45,147
A shadow cannot be called a bridge.

30
00:01:45,227 --> 00:01:49,526
What keeps naming from being
arbitrary is the vividness itself.

31
00:01:50,338 --> 00:01:54,063
Second, concepts are not
a veil over appearance.

32
00:01:54,143 --> 00:01:55,843
The cup is seen as a cup.

33
00:01:55,923 --> 00:01:57,772
The duck is seen as a duck.

34
00:01:57,852 --> 00:02:01,128
Designation, the picking
out and naming of something,

35
00:02:01,208 --> 00:02:05,140
is not a screen held in front of
the world. It is in the appearing.

36
00:02:05,953 --> 00:02:08,047
Neither is the ground of the other.

37
00:02:08,127 --> 00:02:12,109
Concepts draw their content and
their correction from what appears.

38
00:02:12,189 --> 00:02:16,240
What appears is picked out and
made usable through designation.

39
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:19,772
They are designated together,
like long and short.

40
00:02:19,852 --> 00:02:24,202
Even the words conceptual and
non-conceptual depend on each other.

41
00:02:24,282 --> 00:02:27,195
Each is known only as
the other's contrast.

42
00:02:28,013 --> 00:02:30,338
Dreyfus's own examples show this.

43
00:02:30,418 --> 00:02:34,047
Riding a bicycle is not a set
of rules stored in the head.

44
00:02:34,127 --> 00:02:37,348
Nor is it a wordless
reflex beneath all rules.

45
00:02:37,428 --> 00:02:40,052
The skill is designated
together with rider,

46
00:02:40,132 --> 00:02:44,418
machine, slope and wind, and
found in none of them alone.

47
00:02:45,225 --> 00:02:48,066
And designation is wider than language.

48
00:02:48,146 --> 00:02:52,645
To say "conceptual all the way down"
mistakes designation for a kind of

49
00:02:52,725 --> 00:02:53,718
sentence.

50
00:02:53,798 --> 00:02:58,677
To say "non-conceptual at the bottom"
mistakes the absence of words for the

51
00:02:58,757 --> 00:03:00,625
absence of designation.

52
00:03:01,429 --> 00:03:03,781
The ordinary distinction survives.

53
00:03:03,861 --> 00:03:06,552
Seeing a snake is not remembering one.

54
00:03:06,632 --> 00:03:11,512
A perception can correct a thought, as
a lamp shows the snake to be a rope.

55
00:03:11,592 --> 00:03:17,476
Dharmakīrti's line between perception
and conception keeps its everyday use.

56
00:03:17,556 --> 00:03:20,751
What it loses is the
claim to mark two natures,

57
00:03:20,831 --> 00:03:22,467
one beneath the other.

58
00:03:23,273 --> 00:03:26,100
So the answer is neither
concept at the bottom,

59
00:03:26,180 --> 00:03:28,673
nor something without
concept at the bottom.

60
00:03:29,506 --> 00:03:30,721
There is no down.

